  id: 'indra',
  about: 'Indra is the great god of the early Rig Veda — the storm-wielding, Soma-drinking, dragon-slaying king of the gods, to whom more hymns are addressed than to anyone else. This is his most famous hymn, and its architecture is unforgettable: verse after verse describes a mighty deed — he propped up the sky, freed the rivers, slew the dragon, steadies the trembling earth — and each one lands on the same drumbeat refrain, sa janāsa indraḥ: "He, O people — He is Indra." It is a hymn built to be shouted in company, a naming of the god by his works. And then, in the middle of all this triumphant certainty, comes the verse that makes this hymn genuinely astonishing and genuinely modern: the poet reports that some people ask "where is he?" — and some say flatly, "he does not exist." Inside the central praise-hymn to the chief god of the Veda sits a plain acknowledgment that people doubt he is real. That the tradition kept the doubt in the hymn, rather than scrubbing it out, is the most honest thing about it.',
