      commentary: 'The refrain is the whole design: sa janāsa indraḥ — "He, O people, is Indra." Janāsaḥ, "O people," is a direct address to the crowd — this is a hymn meant to be performed aloud, the deeds recited and the assembly answering, each time, with the god\'s name. It is a naming by works: not "Indra is defined as X," but "the one who did this, and this, and this — that one is Indra." The god is known the way we actually come to know anyone who matters: not by a definition but by a gathering of the things they have done. And it begins at the beginning — "the moment he was born, he surpassed the gods." Power that arrives fully formed, that the older order cannot contain. Verse by verse the hymn will pile up his acts until the name at the end of each line stops being a label and starts being a conclusion you are drawn to reach yourself.'
