      transliteration: 'yaṁ smā pṛcchanti kuha seti ghoramutemāhurnaiṣo astītyenam\nso aryaḥ puṣṭīrvija ivā mināti śradasmai dhatta sa janāsa indraḥ',
      translation: 'Of whom, the terrible one, they ask, "Where is he?" — and of whom they even say, "He does not exist"; he sweeps away the foe\'s riches like a gambler\'s winnings. Put your faith in him — He, O people, is Indra.',
      commentary: 'This is the verse that lifts the hymn out of its own time. In the middle of the Rig Veda\'s grandest praise of its chief god, the poet stops and reports, without flinching, what people actually say: kuha sa iti — "where is he?" — and, more bluntly still, na eṣaḥ asti — "he does not exist." Doubt of the god, quoted inside the god\'s own hymn. No other ancient scripture is quite this candid about the unbelief surrounding its own claims. And notice the poet\'s response — he does not threaten the doubters or call down ruin. He answers with an argument from effect ("look what he sweeps away") and then a simple appeal: śrad asmai dhatta — "place your trust in him." Śraddhā, the word that will echo through the Gita and become the whole vocabulary of faith, appears here as an invitation, not a command. The hymn holds the doubt and the faith in the same breath — which is exactly why it reads as honest rather than as propaganda. A tradition confident enough to record the sentence "he does not exist" in its holiest praise-song is a tradition that was never afraid of the question.'
