      transliteration: 'yaṁ krandasī saṁyatī vihvayete pare\'vara ubhayā amitrāḥ\nsamānaṁ cidrathamātasthivāṁsā nānā havete sa janāsa indraḥ',
      translation: 'Whom both clashing armies call upon — the far side and the near, enemies both; whom even two men mounted on the very same chariot invoke, each for himself — He, O people, is Indra.',
      commentary: 'A small, sharp piece of psychological truth is buried in this battle verse, and it is worth drawing out. Both armies pray to Indra — of course; enemies always claim the same god. But the poet pushes the blade in further: even two men standing on one chariot invoke him "each for himself," nānā, separately. Side by side, sharing the same danger and the same vehicle, each prays privately for his own outcome. It is a three-thousand-year-old observation about how petition works — how the same god is asked by everyone for contradictory things, how prayer curves inward toward the self even when we stand shoulder to shoulder. The hymn does not moralize about it; it just notices, with a clear eye, that this is what people do. It is the same unsentimental honesty that let verse five record the doubters. This poet watches human beings closely, and tells the truth about them.'
