      commentary: 'The refrain breaks. For fourteen verses each stanza ended in the third person — "He, O people, is Indra" — the god described to the crowd. The final verse turns and speaks to him directly: sa kila asi satyaḥ — "you, surely, are the real one." After all the deeds recited and all the doubt admitted, the hymn resolves not into proof but into address: it stops talking about Indra and starts talking to him. And notice what answers the doubters of verse five — not an argument, but a relationship. "You are the true one" is said face to face, the way you affirm a person you have decided to trust, not a proposition you have finished demonstrating. The closing wish is the same modest, human one the whole Rig Veda keeps returning to: let us be dear to you, let us have good children, and — the last words — vidatham ā vadema, "may we speak forth in the assembly." Even this hymn to the thunder-god ends where the Veda always ends: with people, together, hoping to have their say among their own.'
